#!/bin/sh
# Refuses a direct push to main.
#
# The control is on the SERVER, not here. The repository is public, and a
# ruleset on main (id 23712687, no bypass actors, so the owner is bound too)
# requires the "verify" and "e2e" checks green on a pull request's head
# commit, allows only squash-merge through a pull request, and refuses direct
# pushes, force-pushes and deletion of main. This hook is a courtesy in front
# of that: it runs on the machine doing the pushing and stops the likeliest
# accident (a `git push` while main happens to be checked out) before the
# server has to, with a message that says what to do instead.
#
# It deliberately does NOT try to be a security control. Anyone who wants
# past it can pass the override below, use --no-verify, or push from a clone
# where the hook is not installed; the ruleset still refuses the push. That
# is the correct scope: this guards against a slip, not against intent.
#
# Install (once per clone, hooks are not carried by git itself):
#
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# Override (skips THIS hook only; with no bypass actors on the ruleset, the
# server refuses a direct push to main anyway):
#
#     bash:       ALLOW_MAIN_PUSH=1 git push
#     PowerShell: $env:ALLOW_MAIN_PUSH=1; git push; Remove-Item Env:ALLOW_MAIN_PUSH

while read -r _local_ref local_sha remote_ref _remote_sha; do
  case "$remote_ref" in
    refs/heads/main) ;;
    *) continue ;;
  esac

  if [ "$ALLOW_MAIN_PUSH" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: ALLOW_MAIN_PUSH is set — allowing a direct push to main." >&2
    continue
  fi

  # An all-zero local sha means "delete the remote branch".
  case "$local_sha" in
    0000000000000000000000000000000000000000)
      echo "pre-push: refusing to DELETE main." >&2
      ;;
    *)
      echo "pre-push: refusing to push directly to main." >&2
      ;;
  esac

  cat >&2 <<'MSG'

main deploys production on every push, and CI runs on branches so a pull
request carries the evidence. Open one instead:

    git switch -c <a-branch-name>
    git push -u origin <a-branch-name>

then squash-merge it on GitHub once the "verify" and "e2e" checks are green
on its head commit. The ruleset on main refuses a direct push regardless of
this hook.

To skip this hook deliberately (the server will still refuse the push):

    bash:       ALLOW_MAIN_PUSH=1 git push
    PowerShell: $env:ALLOW_MAIN_PUSH=1; git push; Remove-Item Env:ALLOW_MAIN_PUSH

MSG
  exit 1
done

exit 0
